54. 594. Td
(The United States Constitution establishes an asymmetric legal environment wherein the sovereign state and private) Tj
T* (platforms operate under fundamentally different speech governance frameworks. Under the State Action Doctrine) Tj
T* (articulated in the Civil Rights Cases \(1883\) and Lugar v. Edmondson Oil Co. \(1982\), the First Amendment operates) Tj
T* (strictly as a negative check on governmental power, rather than a restriction on private commercial entities. When the) Tj
T* (Department of Justice, the FBI, or state law enforcement agencies seek to restrict, penalize, or censor speech) Tj
T* (associated with domestic political extremism, their actions are constrained by strict scrutiny and the incitement test) Tj
T* (articulated in Brandenburg v. Ohio \(1969\).) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 9.5 Tf
10.9249999999999989 TL
0. g
54. 504. Td
(Under Brandenburg, the state cannot proscribe advocacy of the use of force or of law violation except where such) Tj
T* (advocacy is directed to inciting or producing imminent lawless action and is likely to incite or produce such action. This) Tj
T* (constitutional standard protects a wide array of domestic extremist rhetoric. Abstract praise for historical acts of political) Tj
T* (violence, generalized calls for revolution, white supremacist ideology, eco-extremist philosophy, and anti-government) Tj
T* (militia doctrine remain constitutionally protected speech. The Supreme Court reinforced this high evidentiary threshold in) Tj
T* (Counterman v. Colorado \(2023\) and Virginia v. Black \(2003\), requiring that to prosecute true threats, the state must) Tj
T* (establish that the speaker acted with at least a subjective mental state of recklessness regarding the threatening nature) Tj
T* (of their communication, or acted with a specific intent to intimidate.) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 9.5 Tf
10.9249999999999989 TL
0. g
54. 402. Td
(Consequently, the U.S. government cannot establish a formal domestic terrorist organization list without violating First) Tj
T* (Amendment protections concerning freedom of association \(NAACP v. Alabama, 1958\) and viewpoint-based) Tj
T* (discrimination \(R.A.V. v. City of St. Paul, 1992\). Any statutory attempt by the federal government to prohibit membership) Tj
T* (in, or peaceful material support to, a domestic political organization would trigger strict scrutiny, failing the requirement of) Tj
T* (narrow tailoring unless the state can prove an individual's specific intent to further the group's illegal, violent aims) Tj
T* (\(Scales v. United States, 1961\).) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 9.5 Tf
10.9249999999999989 TL
0. g
54. 324. Td
(While the First Amendment restricts state intervention against extremist advocacy, it protects the editorial discretion of) Tj
T* (private platforms. In Moody v. NetChoice, LLC \(2024\), the Supreme Court confirmed that major social media platforms) Tj
T* (and interactive digital services engage in expressive activity protected by the First Amendment when curating,) Tj
T* (organizing, ranking, and moderating third-party content. The Moody Court established that compiling and curating) Tj
T* (third-party speech—deciding what content to include, exclude, prioritize, or demote—constitutes editorial judgment) Tj
T* (analogous to the constitutionally protected choices made by traditional newspapers, parade organizers, and utility) Tj
T* (newsletter publishers \(Miami Herald Publishing Co. v. Tornillo, 1974; Hurley v. Irish-American Gay, Lesbian and Bisexual) Tj
T* (Group of Boston, 1995;) Tj
ET
